<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">16 February in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 16 February, from 1659 to 1968.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/london" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1659 CE <span class="tih-ago">367 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Earliest surviving English cheque is written</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Nicholas Vanacker wrote a cheque for 400 pounds drawn on his London goldsmith-bankers, Morris and Clayton. It is the oldest known surviving handwritten cheque in Britain.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1804 CE <span class="tih-ago">222 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Stephen Decatur burns the captured USS Philadelphia at Tripoli</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Lieutenant Decatur and a small crew slipped into Tripoli harbour and set fire to the frigate, captured by the Barbary state. Nelson is said to have called it the most daring act of the age.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1923 CE <span class="tih-ago">103 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Howard Carter opens Tutankhamun's burial chamber</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Before invited officials, Carter broke through the sealed wall to reveal a gilded shrine filling the chamber. The king's sarcophagus and gold coffins lay within, untouched for over 3,000 years.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1937 CE <span class="tih-ago">89 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Wallace Carothers's nylon is patented</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">DuPont received the patent for nylon, invented by a team led by Wallace Carothers. The first fully synthetic fibre, it was used for stockings and then wartime parachutes.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1945 CE <span class="tih-ago">81 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">American paratroopers and infantry assault Corregidor</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">US forces landed by parachute and sea on the island fortress at the mouth of Manila Bay, lost in 1942. After two weeks of fighting most of its Japanese defenders were dead.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1968 CE <span class="tih-ago">58 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">First 911 emergency call made in Alabama</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In Haleyville, Alabama, Senator Rankin Fite made the first call on the new 911 emergency number. A single, easily remembered number spread across the United States over the following decades.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li></ol>
